**Grammar:** `-- Merged into Bind`

**Semantics:** This entry is deprecated and fully merged into the capitalized Bind construct. It is no longer a separately indexed normative construct.

**Example:**
- NL: "Deprecated."
- BrainCode: `ENTRYPOINT Deprecated

TASK Deprecated {}`

*Introduced/last revised: sprint 2, version 2.0.0*

### `Generate`

**Grammar:** `generation ::= "GENERATE" "(" gen_attr_list? ")" "->" IDENT ":" gen_type
gen_attr_list ::= gen_attr ("," gen_attr)*
gen_attr ::= "quantity" "=" UNSIGNED_INT | IDENT "=" value
UNSIGNED_INT ::= "0" | [1-9][0-9]*
gen_type ::= "NUMBER" | "STRING" | "BOOL" | "LIST" "[" gen_type "]"
-- A quantity attribute, if present, must use the quantity production above; quantity=NUMBER through the generic attribute production is a static error.`

**Semantics:** GENERATE is a pure, non-effectful construct that invokes a generative model to produce a value without external side effects. It shares Action's canonical attribute vocabulary and duplicate-attribute prohibition. target is always the requested output artifact, never content; content is the source instruction or text payload; audience is the intended reader; recipient is forbidden. Its optional tone and register_note follow Action's shared tone normalization rules and are mutually exclusive.

A GENERATE result type must be a gen_type: NUMBER, STRING, BOOL, or a finite nesting of LIST around those types. REF[T] is prohibited at every nesting depth: GENERATE may not declare REF[T], LIST[REF[T]], or any type containing REF. This restriction follows the global REF rule: generated text or values cannot manufacture an opaque runtime identity. The generator must return a value exactly matching its declared gen_type, including homogeneous recursively valid list elements.

quantity is an exact output-size constraint, not a maximum, minimum, estimate, or preference. Its lexical form is UNSIGNED_INT only: it may have no sign, decimal point, exponent, whitespace, or leading zero except for the literal 0. quantity is legal only for results declared STRING or LIST[T]. For LIST[T], the result contains exactly quantity elements. For STRING, it contains exactly quantity words. A word is a maximal non-empty sequence of Unicode characters whose General_Category is a Letter category (L*) or Decimal_Number (Nd); every other Unicode character is a separator. Thus quantity=0 on STRING requires a string containing no such sequence, and quantity=0 on LIST[T] requires an empty list. The executor validates the declared type and quantity condition before completion; inability to produce a conforming result fails the step under the general external-failure rule.

GENERATE must always bind its result using -> IDENT : gen_type. Use GENERATE to produce an artifact for the agent environment; use CONVO and UTTER to record observed dialogue or dialogue history.

**Example:**
- NL: "Give the user an opinion about taxes in exactly 50 words."
- BrainCode: `ENTRYPOINT TaxesOpinion

TASK TaxesOpinion : STRING {
  GENERATE(target="response", audience="user", content="Respond to the user's view that taxes are bad.", quantity=50) -> response : STRING
  RETURN response
}`

*Introduced/last revised: sprint 7, version 6.0.0*


Proposed changes available for this translation:
- [revise] Types & Lexicon: grammar `type ::= NUMBER | BOOL | DATE | TIME | URL | PATH | ATOM | MESSAGE | LIST[type] | REF[type]
value ::= number-literal | TRUE | FALSE | atom-symbol | date-literal | time-literal | url-literal | path-literal | declared-name | message | list-literal | diff
message ::= intent-symbol "(" slot-list? ")"
slot-list ::= semantic-slot "=" value ("," semantic-slot "=" value)*
list-literal ::= "[" value ("," value)* "]"
diff ::= declared-name "-" number-literal
declared-name ::= [A-Z][A-Za-z0-9_]* | [a-z][a-z0-9_]*
number-literal ::= NUMBER lexical form defined in the structural lexicon.` — BrainCode has no STRING type and no quoted-text literal. NUMBER, BOOL, DATE, TIME, URL, PATH, ATOM, MESSAGE, LIST[T], and REF[T] are the only value types. DATE, TIME, URL, and PATH values use only their respective literal-category forms. An atom-symbol is valid only when it is a member of a glossary category whose member type is ATOM. A declared-name is valid only if it is introduced by the declaration or binding position that defines its namespace; it is not an independently meaningful word. MESSAGE is a symbolic semantic frame: its intent-symbol and every semantic-slot must be glossary members, slot order is the canonical order published by the intent entry, and each slot value must satisfy that slot's declared type/category. All keywords, delimiters, operators, type names, and lexical forms in this grammar are structural-lexicon entries. Comments are not part of an expression.
- [add vocabulary] structural-lexicon (closed): Structural-lexicon contains BrainCode's reserved keywords, operators, delimiters, type names, and literal-form names. These symbols are syntax, not descriptive domain words. Members: TASK, ACTION, UTTER, GENERATE, CONVO, IF, FOR, NUMBER, MESSAGE
- [add vocabulary] atomic-literal (open): Atomic-literal defines open-ended values whose exact spelling is not enumerated, such as numbers, dates, times, URLs, paths, and registered proper names. Each literal is one token and never a sentence or phrase. Members: 42, DATE_2025_06_10, NAME_Ada_Lovelace; membership rule: NUMBER is -?(0|[1-9][0-9]*)(.[0-9]+)?([eE][+-]?[0-9]+)?. DATE is DATE_YYYY_MM_DD with a valid Gregorian date. TIME is TIME_hh_mm_ss_Z in UTC. URL is URL_ followed by RFC-3986 percent-encoded ASCII with no whitespace. PATH is PATH_ followed by slash-separated percent-encoded segments with no whitespace. NAME is NAME_ followed by one or more underscore-separated Unicode letter-or-digit components. Each occurrence is one atomic token; no literal form admits spaces, quotation marks, English grammar, or an unstructured phrase.
- [add vocabulary] semantic-symbol (open): Semantic-symbol contains the canonical descriptive symbols used for operations, entities, participants, speech acts, intents, slots, and attribute values. A complete glossary registers every usable symbol in one of these subcategories. Members: request_extension, polite, mug, user, duration; membership rule: A semantic-symbol has lowercase underscore-separated canonical form and is valid only when registered in the complete glossary as exactly one of: operation-symbol, entity-symbol, role-symbol, speech-act-symbol, intent-symbol, semantic-slot, relation-symbol, or attribute-value. Each registered member supplies its category, plain-language definition, canonical slot order where applicable, allowed value type/category, and natural-language synonyms. Symbols cannot be invented from English words at expression-writing time.
- [revise] Task: grammar `document ::= ENTRYPOINT declared-name (task | conversation)+
task ::= TASK declared-name param-list? (CONTEXT execution_date "=" date-literal)? (":" type)? (DECREASES declared-name)? "{" step+ return-stmt? "}"
param-list ::= "(" param ("," param)* ")"
param ::= declared-name ":" type
return-stmt ::= RETURN value
step ::= action | flow-if | flow-for | bind | task-call | generation
task-call ::= declared-name arg-list? ("->" declared-name)?
arg-list ::= "(" arg ("," arg)* ")"
arg ::= semantic-slot "=" value` — ENTRYPOINT, TASK, CONTEXT, execution_date, DECREASES, RETURN, and all punctuation are structural-lexicon members. Task and parameter names are declared-name symbols whose meaning is solely their declaration; calls and references must resolve to an earlier visible declaration. CONTEXT accepts DATE only. Task arguments use registered semantic-slot symbols in the callee's published canonical order. No task declaration, argument, context, or return may contain prose or a quoted string.
- [revise] Action: grammar `action ::= ACTION operation-symbol "(" action-attr-list? ")" ("->" declared-name ":" type)?
action-attr-list ::= action-attr ("," action-attr)*
action-attr ::= semantic-slot "=" value
-- Each operation-symbol glossary entry defines its required and optional semantic-slots, their value categories/types, canonical slot order, and result type/category.` — ACTION performs one registered external operation. The operation-symbol must be a registered operation-symbol, and every supplied slot must be published by that operation's glossary signature; omitted required slots and duplicate slots are static errors. Slots such as target, destination, recipient, deadline, ordering, metric, and meaning accept only their published symbolic types. The former content attribute is removed: an instruction, communication body, query, code change, filter, or read condition is represented by a MESSAGE frame in the meaning slot. Entity references retain REF identity and ordinary entity symbols denote glossary entities; neither is a quoted descriptor. Result binding, SAME/NEW/MEMBER/VALUE behavior, and source-order visibility remain as published per operation.
- [revise] Utterance: grammar `utterance ::= UTTER speech-act-symbol "(" utterance-attr-list ")"
utterance-attr-list ::= recipient "=" role-symbol "," meaning "=" message ("," utterance-modifier)*
utterance-modifier ::= semantic-slot "=" value
-- UTTER is legal only in a CONVO TURN body. Speech-act entries publish permitted modifiers and canonical order.` — UTTER records a non-effectful, symbolic speech act. speech-act-symbol, recipient, meaning, and every modifier are glossary-defined. meaning is mandatory and is a MESSAGE frame; it replaces the former prose content and register_note fields. Tone, register, format, audience, and other modifiers may appear only when permitted by the speech-act entry and must use their registered attribute-value categories. UTTER cannot bind a text string because no text payload exists; a later turn or action must refer structurally through a declared MESSAGE binding if such reuse is needed.
- [revise] Generate: grammar `generation ::= GENERATE "(" generation-attr-list ")" "->" declared-name ":" generation-type
generation-attr-list ::= generation-attr ("," generation-attr)*
generation-attr ::= semantic-slot "=" value
generation-type ::= NUMBER | BOOL | MESSAGE | LIST[generation-type]
-- A generation entry requires target=entity-symbol and meaning=message; quantity, if permitted, is quantity=number-literal.` — GENERATE is a pure request to derive a symbolic value, never unrestricted natural-language text. Its target and meaning are required glossary-governed values. A generator may return NUMBER, BOOL, MESSAGE, or nested lists of those types, but not STRING or REF. The meaning frame specifies the requested artifact semantically; quantity is an exact NUMBER constraint only where the applicable generation profile permits it. Any external rendering of a generated MESSAGE is outside the BrainCode expression and does not become a BrainCode STRING.
- [revise] Bind: grammar `bind ::= LET declared-name ":" type "=" value
-- Additional bind sites use declared-name only: action-result ::= "->" declared-name ":" type ; task-call-result ::= "->" declared-name ; generation-result ::= "->" declared-name ":" generation-type.` — Bind introduces a declared-name into its lexical scope. The name is valid because its binding occurrence defines it, and later uses must resolve to that declaration. LET can bind only a value in the revised closed type system; it cannot introduce prose, a quoted descriptor, or an undeclared symbol. REF copying, no-shadowing, exact type matching, and lexical visibility remain unchanged.
- [revise] Conversation: grammar `conversation ::= CONVO declared-name "{" turn+ "}"
turn ::= TURN declared-name SPEAKER "=" speaker-symbol (REPLY_TO declared-name)? (REVISES declared-name)? "{" conversation-step+ "}"
conversation-step ::= action | utterance | flow-if | flow-for | bind | task-call | generation
speaker-symbol ::= USER | AGENT` — CONVO, TURN, SPEAKER, REPLY_TO, REVISES, USER, and AGENT are structural-lexicon symbols. Conversation and turn names are declared-name symbols introduced by their declarations; REPLY_TO and REVISES must resolve to earlier declared turn names in the same conversation. Turn content uses revised UTTER and ACTION forms, so no dialogue body contains quoted natural language. The linear revision-chain and isolated turn-scope rules remain unchanged.
- [revise] Check: grammar `check ::= disjunction
disjunction ::= conjunction (OR conjunction)*
conjunction ::= negation (AND negation)*
negation ::= NOT negation | primary
primary ::= "(" check ")" | quantifier | comparison
comparison ::= value (comp-op value)?
comp-op ::= == | != | < | <= | > | >=` — Check remains a pure boolean expression over revised closed values. OR, AND, NOT, comparison operators, parentheses, and quantifier keywords are structural-lexicon members. Every non-structural token in a Check must be a visible declared-name, a glossary semantic symbol, or an atomic literal under Types & Lexicon. MESSAGE values support equality and inequality only; ordering is permitted only for NUMBER, DATE, TIME, URL, and PATH where their literal category defines an ordering.
- [revise] Iterate: grammar `flow-for ::= FOR EACH declared-name IN iterable "{" step+ "}"
iterable ::= declared-name | list-literal
quantifier ::= (ANY | ALL) declared-name IN iterable SATISFIES check` — FOR, EACH, IN, ANY, ALL, and SATISFIES are structural-lexicon symbols. The iteration and quantifier names are declared-name bindings scoped exactly as before. List elements must be closed revised values; a list cannot contain quoted prose or undeclared identifiers. Sequential evaluation, finite-list termination, type checking, and no-shadowing remain unchanged.
- [revise] Flow-If: grammar `flow-if ::= IF check THEN "{" step+ "}" (ELSE "{" step+ "}")?` — IF, THEN, and ELSE are structural-lexicon symbols. Flow-If branches only on a revised closed Check and executes revised closed steps. Its scope, deferred external-check behavior, and branch execution rules remain unchanged; any action or utterance in a branch must satisfy the revised symbolic payload rules.

